Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage for this task  
• "project_short_name": filters the BRCA project (“TCGA‑BRCA”).  
• "case_barcode": identifies an individual case; lets us “collect” all samples that belong to that patient.  
• "sample_type_name": tells us which tissue a sample came from (e.g. “Solid Tissue Normal”, “Primary Tumor” …).  
• "gene_type": lets us keep only rows where the feature is “protein_coding”.  
• "sample_barcode" / "aliquot_barcode": useful when we need to drill down to individual samples or aliquots after finding the correct cases.  
• "gene_name", "Ensembl_gene_id": gene identifiers if we eventually want to view expression values.  
• "fpkm_unstranded", "tpm_unstranded", "unstranded" … : expression measures that might be displayed after we know which cases qualify.  

Basic conditions that will be reused  
1) "project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'  
2) "gene_type" = 'protein_coding'  
3) The same "case_barcode" must contain more than one distinct "sample_type_name" and one of those must be “Solid Tissue Normal”.

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL statements (each limited to 20 rows) that progressively build toward the final answer.  
All names are enclosed in double quotes and each query is preceded by a brief inline comment.

```sql
-- 1) Distinct tissue‑type labels available for TCGA‑BRCA
SELECT DISTINCT "sample_type_name"
FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35"
WHERE "project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Sample‑type distribution inside one randomly chosen BRCA case
SELECT DISTINCT "case_barcode",
       "sample_type_name"
FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35"
WHERE "project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'
  AND "case_barcode" = (  -- pick the first BRCA case alphabetically
        SELECT MIN("case_barcode")
        FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35"
        WHERE "project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'
      )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) How many distinct tissue types does each BRCA case have?
SELECT "case_barcode",
       COUNT(DISTINCT "sample_type_name") AS "n_types"
FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35"
WHERE "project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'
GROUP BY "case_barcode"
ORDER BY "n_types" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) BRCA cases that contain at least one 'Solid Tissue Normal' sample
SELECT DISTINCT "case_barcode"
FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35"
WHERE "project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'
  AND "sample_type_name" ILIKE '%Solid%Tissue%Normal%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) BRCA cases that have >1 tissue type AND contain 'Solid Tissue Normal'
SELECT "case_barcode"
FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35"
WHERE "project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'
GROUP BY "case_barcode"
HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "sample_type_name") > 1
   AND SUM(CASE WHEN "sample_type_name" ILIKE '%Solid%Tissue%Normal%' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Same as #5 but listing all tissue types for each qualifying case
SELECT "case_barcode",
       ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT "sample_type_name") AS "all_tissue_types"
FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35"
WHERE "project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'
GROUP BY "case_barcode"
HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "sample_type_name") > 1
   AND SUM(CASE WHEN "sample_type_name" ILIKE '%Solid%Tissue%Normal%' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) > 0
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Distinct samples (sample_barcode) belonging to those multi‑tissue cases
SELECT DISTINCT r."case_barcode",
                r."sample_barcode",
                r."sample_type_name"
FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35" r
JOIN (
      SELECT "case_barcode"
      FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35"
      WHERE "project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'
      GROUP BY "case_barcode"
      HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "sample_type_name") > 1
         AND SUM(CASE WHEN "sample_type_name" ILIKE '%Solid%Tissue%Normal%' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) > 0
     ) q
  ON r."case_barcode" = q."case_barcode"
WHERE r."project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Gene‑type breakdown (should confirm 'protein_coding' dominates but shows others)
SELECT "gene_type",
       COUNT(*) AS "n_rows"
FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35"
WHERE "project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'
GROUP BY "gene_type"
ORDER BY "n_rows" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) For a qualifying case: pull a small slice of expression for protein_coding genes only
SELECT "case_barcode",
       "sample_barcode",
       "gene_name",
       "tpm_unstranded"
FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35"
WHERE "project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'
  AND "gene_type" = 'protein_coding'
  AND "case_barcode" IN (  -- reuse criteria from #5
        SELECT "case_barcode"
        FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35"
        WHERE "project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'
        GROUP BY "case_barcode"
        HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "sample_type_name") > 1
           AND SUM(CASE WHEN "sample_type_name" ILIKE '%Solid%Tissue%Normal%' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) > 0
      )
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Comprehensive pull: list each qualifying BRCA case with its tissue types, sample counts and example protein_coding record
WITH qualified_cases AS (
    SELECT "case_barcode"
    FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35"
    WHERE "project_short_name" = 'TCGA-BRCA'
    GROUP BY "case_barcode"
    HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT "sample_type_name") > 1
       AND SUM(CASE WHEN "sample_type_name" ILIKE '%Solid%Tissue%Normal%' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) > 0
)
SELECT q."case_barcode",
       COUNT(DISTINCT r."sample_barcode") AS "n_samples",
       ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT r."sample_type_name") AS "tissue_types",
       MAX(CASE WHEN r."gene_type" = 'protein_coding' THEN r."gene_name" END) AS "example_gene"
FROM "TCGA"."TCGA_VERSIONED"."RNASEQ_HG38_GDC_R35" r
JOIN qualified_cases q
  ON r."case_barcode" = q."case_barcode"
WHERE r."gene_type" = 'protein_coding'
GROUP BY q."case_barcode"
LIMIT 20;
```

